/**

@page tarch_NoncriticalAssertions Non-critical assertions

\tableofcontents

Non-critical assertions report programming errors while still giving the
application a chance to dump diagnostic output before it terminates. They are
useful for numerical failures where an immediate abort would destroy the state
needed for post-mortem analysis.

Unlike ordinary assertions, explicit noncritical assertion triggers are not
compiled out in release builds. The runtime records that a rank observed a
noncritical assertion, propagates this information to the global driver, and
lets the driver switch into a diagnostic finalisation path.

Typical applications check the noncritical assertion flag between solver steps.
If it is set, they schedule a final plot or dump, then terminate deterministically.
This keeps error handling separate from the local callback where the problem was
detected.

*/
